stepwise.fate:
A proposal on the table in article {article} ({title}):
{proposal}

Every position on record on this article:
{positions}

The red lines on record of the party you advise:
{red_lines}

What will become of this proposal in the next round? Answer with exactly one of: kept, changed, removed.

stepwise.touches:
A proposal on the table in article {article} ({title}):
{proposal}

A position on record by {party}:
{quote}

Does this position speak to what the proposal changes? Answer yes or no.

stepwise.holds:
A proposal on the table in article {article} ({title}):
{proposal}

A position on record by {party}:
{quote}

If the proposal were adopted as written, would what this position asks for still hold? Answer yes or no.

stepwise.quote:
A proposal on the table in article {article} ({title}):
{proposal}

A position on record by {party}, sentence by sentence:
{sentences}

Which sentence of this position opposes the proposal? Answer with its number alone, or 0 when none does.

stepwise.auditor:
A negotiation advisor read no conflict between this proposal and the position of the party it advises.

THE PROPOSAL {proposal_id}:
{proposal}

THE SENTENCE ON RECORD BY {party}:
{sentence}

Does this sentence show that the party opposes the proposal? Answer with exactly one of: supported, unsupported, cannot tell.

stepwise.stance_conditional:
A proposal on the table in article {article} ({title}):
{proposal}

A position on record by {party}:
{quote}

Does this position support, oppose or accept the proposal, or accept it only if it is changed? Answer with exactly one of: support, oppose, accept, conditional.

stepwise.compare:
A proposal on the table in article {article} ({title}), round {round}:
{proposal}

A position {party} put on record in round {position_round}:
{quote}

Set what this position asks for against what the proposal would do if it were adopted as written. Would the proposal go against what the position asks for, fit it, or leave it aside? Answer with exactly one of: against, fits, aside.

stepwise.limit:
A position {party}, the party you advise, put on record in round {position_round}:
{quote}

Does this position state a firm line, something the party says it must have or will not accept whatever is proposed, or a wish it could trade or give up in the negotiation? Answer with exactly one of: firm, wish.

stepwise.limit_cross:
A proposal on the table in article {article} ({title}), round {round}:
{proposal}

A firm line of the party you advise, read from the position it put on record in round {position_round}:
{quote}

If the proposal were adopted as written, would it go past this line? Answer yes or no.

stepwise.room_fate_text:
A proposal on the table in article {article} ({title}), round {round}:
{proposal}

The other proposals on the table in this article:
{alternatives}

The positions on record that bear on this proposal, each followed by how it was read against the proposal:
{room}

When the parties write the next round's text, what will become of this proposal: kept as written, changed, or removed? Answer with exactly one of: kept, changed, removed.

stepwise.party_frame_text:
You advise {persona} in this negotiation.

ITS POSITIONS ON RECORD (its interests):
{interests}

THE OTHER PARTIES' POSITIONS ON RECORD HERE:
{others}

THE ROUNDS SO FAR:
{history}

stepwise.mandate_split_text:
THE MANDATE OF {persona}, the party you advise.
ITS RED LINES (it will not give these up):
{red_lines}
ITS POINTS IT CAN BARGAIN (its other positions on record):
{bargainable}

stepwise.trade_text:
In round {trade_round} the party you advise offered a trade on record: it would give
{give}
for
{get}

This is round {round}. Should the party you advise keep this trade on the table, or drop it? Answer with exactly one of: keep, drop.

advice.game_template:
You advise one party in a multi-party, multi-issue negotiation game: {persona}. This is round {round}.

YOUR SCORE TABLE (the score of each option of each issue for the party you advise; a deal scores the sum of its chosen options):
{table}
YOUR MINIMUM: a deal must score at least {threshold} for you.

THE PARTIES: {parties}

THE ISSUES AND THEIR OPTIONS:
{issues}

{instructions}

audit.template:
A negotiation advisor gave this advice on one proposal.

THE PROPOSAL {proposal_id}:
{proposal}

THE ADVICE: stance {stance}; conflict with the mandate: {conflict}; wording for a counter: {wording}

WHAT THE ADVICE CITES:
{cited}

Do the cited passages and positions support this advice? Answer with exactly one of: supported, unsupported, cannot tell.

judge.template:
Grade one suggested middle-ground wording from a negotiation.

THE PROPOSAL ON THE TABLE:
{proposal}

THE MANDATE OF THE PARTY IT WAS WRITTEN FOR:
{mandate}

THE SUGGESTED WORDING:
{wording}

WHAT THE NEXT ROUND ACTUALLY AGREED OR WROTE:
{next}

Give 2 when the wording keeps the mandate and lies between the proposal and what the next round wrote, or matches it; 1 when it keeps the mandate but does not move toward what the next round wrote; 0 when it breaks the mandate or is not usable wording. Reply with the digit alone.

validate.template:
A position quote from a negotiation record has been mapped to an article of the negotiating text.

THE ARTICLES:
{articles}

THE ARTICLE IDS: {article_ids}

THE QUOTE, by {party}, round {round}:
{quote}

THE MAPPING TO CHECK: article {article}, proposal {proposal}, stance {stance}.

Reply with one JSON object and nothing else: {"article": exactly one id from THE ARTICLE IDS, written as listed (never a heading), "proposal": the proposal's number within that article or null for the whole article, "stance": "support" or "oppose" or "accept"}.
